<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Rarotonga</h1><p class="st-meta">1858 CE – 1893 CE · capital Avarua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A chiefly kingdom on the main island of the Cook Islands, led by the Makea line, which sought British protection and became the core of the Cook Islands Federation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rarotonga was divided among tribes led by hereditary ariki, including the Makea, Pa, Kainuku and Tinomana titles. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from 1823, through the Tahitian teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeiha">Papeiha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a>, the ariki governed with mission backing, and the Makea title holders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avarua">Avarua</a> came to be regarded by outsiders as monarchs of the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ariki ruled through chiefly councils and mission-inspired law codes enforced by church judges. The island traded fruit, copra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tahiti">Tahiti</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makea-takau-ariki">Makea Takau Ariki</a>, who held her title from 1871, was treated by visiting ships and governments as queen. Details of the kingdom's formal institutions before the 1880s are thinly documented.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Fearing French annexation, the ariki requested British protection, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was proclaimed in 1888. The British Resident <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-moss">Frederick Moss</a> set up a federal parliament, and Rarotonga became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a> in the early 1890s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-rarotonga.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Rarotonga" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. R-41~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rarotonga_1858-1888.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1858 CE – 1893 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avarua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Makea Takau Ariki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cook Islands Māori</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cook Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rarotonga</dd></dl></aside></div>
